#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""عشرون قصة — ten Maupassant and ten Chekhov stories, retold in spoken Palestinian, advanced.

Guy de Maupassant died in 1893 and Anton Chekhov in 1904; both are long out of copyright, and the
originals are French and Russian. These are retellings from the plots, so no English translation
is involved either — worth stating, because many modern translations of Chekhov are still in
copyright even though Chekhov is not.

WHY THESE CLOSE THE SHELF. Phase 6's milestone is being warm, funny and diplomatic — recognizably
yourself. Holmes trains following an argument; these train the other half: what people do about
pride, money, embarrassment and loneliness, and what they say instead of what they mean. A woman
ruins her life over a necklace she was too proud to ask about. A man is believed to be a thief and
dies still explaining. A cabman whose son has died cannot find one person who will listen, and
tells the horse.

Nothing here has a detective or a moral. That is the point at this level: the reader has to decide
what happened, and Arabic gives them no more help than French or Russian did.

One story per chapter, each complete in a sitting. Maupassant first, then Chekhov, and each half
runs from the lighter stories to the heavier ones.

LEVEL. Written near the advanced short-story baseline (34 characters a sentence), and deliberately
watching the 90th percentile — the Holmes book needed twelve long sentences split after the fact,
so these were written in shorter beats from the start.

As everywhere in this project the PROSE is written by Claude (flagged NOT native-validated), but
every WORD's root, meaning and pronunciation is looked up in Maknuune by the ingest pipeline.

Run:  python3 pipeline/book_shortstories.py    then ingest each chapter + build_app.py.
"""
import os, sys
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
